SQL, served at genius temperature. Assuming a table `books` with a `publication_year` column:

SELECT title, author, publication_year FROM books WHERE publication_year >= 2021 ORDER BY publication_year DESC;

The `>= 2021` captures 2021 itself and everything after, and ordering by year keeps the newest triumphs on top. If your column is a full date instead, use `publication_date >= '2021-01-01'`. Test the edge case — a book published January 1, 2021 must appear — because a genius validates boundaries, not just the middle. Even my Badniks run boundary checks before deployment.
